\title{Integrated Information as a Finite Minimum-Cut Functional}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 2, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a finite mathematical framework for integrated information based on nontrivial bipartitions of a system. A system assigns a nonnegative effective-information value to every subset of its components, and its integrated information $\Phi$ is the minimum over all nonempty proper subsets. We establish the elementary order theory of this functional: the admissible family is nonempty for systems with at least two components, a minimum-information partition exists, $\Phi$ is bounded above by every cut value, every common lower bound of the cut values bounds $\Phi$ from below, and $\Phi$ is nonnegative. We then specialize effective information to the total nonnegative directed interaction weight crossing from a subset to its complement. In this model, $\Phi$ is exactly a directed minimum-cut functional. We prove that $\Phi>0$ if and only if every nontrivial cut carries positive outgoing interaction. Finally, we prove that pointwise multiplicative approximation of every cut transfers with the same factor to the minimum. Exhaustive algorithms, examples, interpretations, limitations, and extensions are discussed.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Integrated-information approaches seek to quantify the degree to which a system behaves as an irreducible whole. Their central intuition is partition-based: divide the system, measure what informational relation is lost, and identify the partition that disrupts the system least. The least disruptive partition is decisive because it exposes the weakest interface.

This paper isolates a finite mathematical core of that idea. We do not prescribe a biological interpretation of effective information. Instead, we begin with an abstract nonnegative value assigned to each candidate cut. This separates the universal order-theoretic behavior of minimization from the domain-specific construction of the cut value.

For a finite system with at least two components, nontrivial bipartitions exist and an actual minimum-information partition realizes the minimum. This supplies upper- and lower-bound principles and nonnegativity. We then specialize to weighted directed networks. Each ordered pair carries a nonnegative interaction weight, and the effective information of a subset is the total interaction leaving it. Integrated information then becomes a directed global minimum cut. Its strict positivity has an exact characterization: every nonempty proper subset must have positive total outward interaction.

We also address approximation. Uniform pointwise multiplicative control of all cut values passes unchanged through minimization. This result is independent of how a surrogate is computed and gives a correctness interface for approximation methods.

The framework is structural. It does not establish NP-hardness, which would require an encoded decision problem and a reduction. Nor does it claim that weighted connectivity alone captures consciousness. It states precisely what follows from the finite minimum-partition construction.

\section{Finite systems and admissible partitions}

Let $n\geq 2$ and let
\[
V=\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}
\]
be the component set. A subset $A\subseteq V$ induces the ordered bipartition $(A,V\setminus A)$.

\begin{definition}[Admissible cut]
A subset $A\subseteq V$ is admissible if $\varnothing\neq A\subsetneq V$. The family of admissible cuts is
\[
\cuts(V)=\{A\subseteq V:A\neq\varnothing\text{ and }A\neq V\}.
\]
\end{definition}

Representing a bipartition by $A$ rather than an unordered pair matters for directed systems: $A$ measures flow from $A$ to its complement, while the complement measures the reverse direction.

\begin{lemma}[Existence of admissible partitions]
If $n\geq2$, then $\cuts(V)$ is nonempty.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Choose $v\in V$. The singleton $\{v\}$ is nonempty. Since $V$ has at least two elements, $\{v\}\neq V$, so it is proper and admissible.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Finite effective-information system]
A finite effective-information system is a pair $(V,E)$, where
\[
E:2^V\longrightarrow\R
\]
satisfies $E(A)\geq0$ for every $A\subseteq V$.
\end{definition}

Only admissible values enter the integrated-information functional.

\begin{definition}[Integrated information]
The integrated information of $(V,E)$ is
\[
\Phif(E)=\min_{A\in\cuts(V)}E(A).
\]
An admissible $A_*$ satisfying $E(A_*)=\Phif(E)$ is a minimum-information partition.
\end{definition}

The definition is a bottleneck principle. Large $\Phif(E)$ requires every admissible split to have large effective information; one weak partition bounds the whole system from above.

\section{Fundamental properties}

\begin{theorem}[Cut upper bound]
For every admissible subset $A$,
\[
\Phif(E)\leq E(A).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The minimum of a nonempty finite collection is no greater than any member. Since $A$ is admissible, $E(A)$ is among the minimized values.
\end{proof}

Thus one displayed cut certifies an upper bound.

\begin{theorem}[Minimum-Information Partition Theorem]
Every finite effective-information system with at least two components has an admissible subset $A_*$ such that
\[
E(A_*)=\Phif(E).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The admissible family is nonempty and finite. Its image under $E$ is therefore a nonempty finite set of real numbers and has a least element. Any preimage of that element is the required $A_*$.
\end{proof}

Attainment distinguishes the finite theory from an infinite infimum problem, in which positive values could approach zero without a zero member.

\begin{theorem}[Common lower-bound principle]
Let $b\in\R$. If $b\leq E(A)$ for every $A\in\cuts(V)$, then
\[
b\leq\Phif(E).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose a minimum-information partition $A_*$. The hypothesis gives $b\leq E(A_*)=\Phif(E)$.
\end{proof}

Unlike an upper bound, a lower bound must control every cut.

\begin{corollary}[Nonnegativity]
Every finite effective-information system satisfies $\Phif(E)\geq0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Use the common lower-bound principle with $b=0$ and the assumed nonnegativity of $E$.
\end{proof}

\section{Directed weighted interaction systems}

Let
\[
w:V\times V\longrightarrow\R_{\geq0}
\]
be a directed weight function. The value $w_{ij}$ represents interaction from $i$ to $j$; symmetry is not assumed. Self-weights do not cross any cut.

\begin{definition}[Outgoing cut weight]
For $A\subseteq V$, define
\[
C_w(A)=\sum_{i\in A}\sum_{j\in V\setminus A}w_{ij}.
\]
\end{definition}

Every summand is nonnegative, so $C_w(A)\geq0$. Hence $E_w(A)=C_w(A)$ defines an effective-information system.

\begin{definition}[Weighted integrated information]
The integrated information induced by $w$ is
\[
\Phif_w=\min_{\varnothing\neq A\subsetneq V}C_w(A).
\]
\end{definition}

This is a directed global minimum-cut functional.

\begin{definition}[Cut-connectivity]
The network is cut-connected if
\[
C_w(A)>0
\]
for every $\varnothing\neq A\subsetneq V$.
\end{definition}

Since weights are nonnegative, $C_w(A)>0$ exactly when some ordered pair $(i,j)$ with $i\in A$ and $j\notin A$ has $w_{ij}>0$. Thus no nonempty proper group is closed against every positive-weight outgoing interaction.

Define the support digraph to contain $i\to j$ exactly when $w_{ij}>0$. Strong connectivity implies that every nontrivial subset has an outgoing support arc. Conversely, if every nontrivial subset has an outgoing arc, the condensation into strongly connected components cannot have more than one vertex: a finite acyclic condensation with at least two vertices has a sink, whose union has no outgoing arc. Thus cut-connectivity agrees with strong connectivity of the positive support graph. We retain cut language because it directly matches the functional.

\section{Integrated information and connectivity}

\begin{theorem}[Integrated Information--Connectivity Theorem]
Let $n\geq2$, and suppose $w_{ij}\geq0$ for every $i,j\in V$. Then
\[
\Phif_w>0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
C_w(A)>0\text{ for every }\varnothing\neq A\subsetneq V.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $\Phif_w>0$. For every admissible $A$, the cut upper bound gives $\Phif_w\leq C_w(A)$, hence $C_w(A)>0$.

Conversely, suppose every admissible cut is positive. Choose an admissible $A_*$ attaining the minimum. Then $\Phif_w=C_w(A_*)>0$.
\end{proof}

Nonnegativity is essential to the support interpretation: signed weights could cancel. Finiteness is essential to the reverse proof because it supplies a minimizer.

\begin{corollary}[Zero-integration certificate]
Under the theorem's assumptions, $\Phif_w=0$ if and only if there exists a nonempty proper $A$ with $C_w(A)=0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
All cut values and their minimum are nonnegative. Failure of strict positivity is therefore equality with zero. Negating cut-connectivity gives an admissible cut with nonpositive value, which nonnegativity forces to be zero. Conversely, a zero cut bounds $\Phif_w$ above by zero and nonnegativity bounds it below.
\end{proof}

A zero value therefore has an interpretable witness: a subsystem with no positive outgoing interaction.

\begin{corollary}[Bottleneck interpretation]
If $A_*$ is a minimum-information partition, then $\Phif_w=C_w(A_*)$. Strengthening interactions that cross no minimum cut need not increase $\Phif_w$.
\end{corollary}

The result warns against replacing integration by total network weight. Integration is controlled by bottlenecks, not aggregate activity.

\section{Multiplicative approximation transfer}

Let $(V,E)$ and $(V,\widetilde E)$ be systems on the same components. Regard $E$ as a target and $\widetilde E$ as a surrogate.

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicative Approximation Transfer Theorem]
Suppose that for every admissible $A$,
\[
E(A)\leq\widetilde E(A)\leq cE(A).
\]
Then
\[
\Phif(E)\leq\Phif(\widetilde E)\leq c\Phif(E).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $B_*$ minimize $\widetilde E$. Then
\[
\Phif(E)\leq E(B_*)\leq\widetilde E(B_*)=\Phif(\widetilde E).
\]
Next let $A_*$ minimize $E$. Since the surrogate minimum is no larger than its value at $A_*$,
\[
\Phif(\widetilde E)\leq\widetilde E(A_*)\leq cE(A_*)=c\Phif(E).
\]
\end{proof}

The minimizing cuts may differ; only the optimum values are controlled. In customary applications $c\geq1$. If $\Phif(E)=0$, nonnegativity and the conclusion force $\Phif(\widetilde E)=0$.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Exhaustive search}

For a system supplied by an evaluation rule $E(A)$, enumerate all nonempty proper subsets, evaluate each, and retain the smallest value and a witness. There are $2^n-2$ candidates. If one evaluation costs $T_E(n)$, the running time is
\[
O(2^nT_E(n)).
\]
For a dense weight matrix, direct cut evaluation costs $O(n^2)$, yielding $O(n^2 2^n)$ time and $O(n)$ auxiliary space apart from the input.

The exact algorithm is:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item Require $n\geq2$ and an $n\times n$ nonnegative matrix $w$.
\item Initialize the best value to $+\infty$ and the witness to none.
\item For each bit mask from $1$ through $2^n-2$:
  \begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
  \item Decode it as a nonempty proper subset $A$.
  \item Sum $w_{ij}$ over $i\in A$ and $j\notin A$.
  \item Replace the best pair if the new value is smaller.
  \end{enumerate}
\item Return the best value and witness.
\end{enumerate}

Correctness follows because each admissible subset appears exactly once. Directed cuts require retaining both $A$ and its complement because their weights may differ.

\subsection{Qualitative connectivity testing}

Exhaustive search decides positivity by checking whether the minimum exceeds zero. A faster qualitative method constructs the support digraph and tests strong connectivity by traversing the graph and its transpose. With adjacency lists this costs $O(n+m)$, where $m$ is the number of positive arcs. It decides whether $\Phif_w>0$ but does not compute its magnitude.

\subsection{Surrogate pipeline}

The approximation theorem suggests a modular procedure:
\begin{enumerate}
\item construct a tractable surrogate $\widetilde E$;
\item prove $E(A)\leq\widetilde E(A)\leq cE(A)$ for every admissible $A$;
\item optimize $\widetilde E$; and
\item transfer the factor guarantee to $\Phif(E)$.
\end{enumerate}
The theorem certifies the final step; construction, runtime, and pointwise analysis remain obligations of a concrete method.

\section{Numerical examples}

\begin{example}[Directed cycle]
Let $V=\{0,1,2\}$ and assign weight $1$ to $0\to1$, $1\to2$, and $2\to0$, with all others zero. Every admissible subset has an outgoing cycle edge. Enumeration gives $\Phif_w=1>0$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Broken cycle]
Remove $2\to0$. For $A=\{2\}$, $C_w(A)=0$, so $\Phif_w=0$. Other activity remains, but $\{2\}$ is a zero-integration certificate.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Weighted bottleneck]
Suppose two dense modules are joined outward by total capacity $\varepsilon>0$. The separating cut gives $0<\Phif_w\leq\varepsilon$ when all cuts remain positive. If every other cut has weight at least $\varepsilon$, equality holds. Internal weight can remain large while integration approaches zero.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Surrogate scaling]
If $\widetilde E(A)=1.5E(A)$ for every admissible $A$, then $\Phif(\widetilde E)=1.5\Phif(E)$. If instead each surrogate cut lies anywhere between $E(A)$ and $1.5E(A)$, minimizers may differ, but
\[
\Phif(E)\leq\Phif(\widetilde E)\leq1.5\Phif(E).
\]
\end{example}

\section{Applications and interpretation}

The model applies wherever directed couplings are represented by nonnegative capacities. In neural interaction models, a cut may represent a candidate subsystem boundary. In communication systems, $\Phif_w$ identifies the least outgoing capacity of a nontrivial group. In organizational or ecological networks, a minimizing partition identifies a module with weak outward coupling.

These share mathematics, not empirical semantics. The choice $E(A)=C_w(A)$ assumes additive pairwise interactions and omits temporal dynamics, interventions, probability distributions, and higher-order effects. Thus $\Phif_w$ is a network integration functional. Identifying it with consciousness would require independent scientific assumptions and evidence.

Direction also matters. One could instead use
\[
C_w^{\mathrm{sym}}(A)=\sum_{i\in A}\sum_{j\notin A}(w_{ij}+w_{ji})
\]
or identify complementary sets as one unordered partition. For symmetric weights, $C_w(A)=C_w(V\setminus A)$; for directed weights these are genuinely different conventions.

\section{Complexity boundary and limitations}

The $2^n-2$ candidates make naive enumeration exponential, but this is not an NP-hardness proof. Complexity requires a precisely encoded problem. One must specify input bit strings, exact optimization versus threshold decision, arithmetic and precision costs, normalization, and a polynomial-time reduction.

For the weighted-cut specialization, graph algorithms exploit structure. Symmetric nonnegative global minimum cut is polynomial-time computable, and directed variants also have specialized methods under standard encodings. An unqualified NP-hardness claim is therefore unsupported for this model. Hardness may arise in richer semantics, but it must be proved after fixing them.

Likewise, approximation transfer does not itself construct a polynomial-time approximation. It says that a tractable surrogate with pointwise factor bounds would transfer those bounds. Runtime and surrogate construction are separate.

Conceptually, the abstract model assumes no complement symmetry, submodularity, monotonicity, or normalization. The weighted model is pairwise and additive and does not model interventions, stochastic repertoires, or information divergences. These omissions keep the established conclusions transparent.

\section{Future work}

A probabilistic extension could define effective information through divergences between repertoires generated by finite Markov kernels. This requires interventions, subsystem marginalization, and a divergence such as total variation or Kullback--Leibler divergence, together with nonnegativity and data-processing results.

A complexity layer should define bit-size-aware encodings of Boolean transition systems and distributions, formulate a threshold problem for normalized integrated information, and prove a reduction from a standard hard problem. This would distinguish the cost of partition search from the cost of evaluating information.

Approximation research should instantiate the transfer theorem by constructing a surrogate, proving pointwise bounds, and analyzing optimization runtime. Alternative directed, symmetric, unordered, and multipartition conventions should be compared. Finally, $\Phif_w$ can be related quantitatively to edge connectivity, conductance, spectral gaps, and stability under perturbations.

\section{Conclusion}

For a finite system, integrated information defined by minimization over nontrivial cuts is attained and nonnegative. Its minimum-information partition is an explicit bottleneck witness. For nonnegative directed crossing weights, $\Phif>0$ exactly when every nontrivial subset sends positive interaction to its complement. Pointwise multiplicative approximation of all cuts transfers unchanged to the minimum.

The organizing principle is simple: integration is governed by the easiest separation. The results also mark a boundary. Connectivity and approximation transfer hold in the finite model; complexity classifications and richer causal semantics require additional definitions and arguments.

